  note    the names are Python's, `sha512_256`, `sha3_256`; Go's `String` writes `SHA-512/256`, which `parse_algorithm` does not read, and Python's `new` also takes upper case. Every algorithm is always available here, so Go's `Available` and Python's two sets are the variant's cases. Since 2026-11-17 the four SHA-3 digests are cases of `Algorithm` too, their block their rate (144, 136, 104, 72) as Python's `block_size` answers, so the HMAC, HKDF and PBKDF2 over SHA-3 come from the same functions; Python's `hmac` and `pbkdf2_hmac` answered them alike. Go's `BLAKE2b_256` and its kin name a digest of `golang.org/x/crypto`, outside the standard library; BLAKE2 has its own functions here, below

