capability crypto/ciphers  encryption, decryption, signatures and key agreement
  heroes  refused: a cipher must compute in a time its key and its text do not decide, and nothing in this wave proves that of the C the compiler makes; a digest needs no such proof
  go      crypto.Signer, crypto.SignerOpts, crypto.SignMessage, crypto.MessageSigner, crypto.Decrypter, crypto.DecrypterOpts, crypto.Encapsulator, crypto.Decapsulator, crypto.PrivateKey, crypto.PublicKey, subtle.ConstantTimeByteEq, subtle.ConstantTimeEq, subtle.ConstantTimeLessOrEq, subtle.ConstantTimeSelect, subtle.ConstantTimeCopy, subtle.XORBytes, subtle.WithDataIndependentTiming
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's packages `crypto/aes`, `cipher`, `des`, `rc4`, `rsa`, `dsa`, `ecdsa`, `ed25519`, `ecdh`, `elliptic`, `mlkem`, `tls` and `x509` stand here whole; `subtle`'s selections serve the writing of such code. Refused for a cipher written in Heroes, and only for that: since 2026-11-17 `crypto/seal` (AES-128-GCM, AES-256-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) and `crypto/keypairs` (Ed25519, ECDSA on P-256 and P-384, RSA-PSS and PKCS #1 v1.5 signatures, RSA-OAEP, X25519 and ECDH, keys as PKCS #8, SPKI, PEM and raw bytes) give these capabilities from C, never from Heroes code: the system's own cryptography, Linux's OpenSSL 3 offering them all; on macOS the Security framework's `SecKey` for RSA and the NIST curves, CryptoKit for sealing through the platform layer's member written in Swift (CommonCrypto has no authenticated mode, CryptoKit is Swift's alone), and for Ed25519 and X25519 Monocypher 4.0.3, a C library the runtime vendors (`SecKey` takes no Curve25519 key, CryptoKit's Ed25519 signs differently every time), since 2026-11-20 (panel 253's R3, decision 2594); Windows' CNG has no Ed25519, and its X25519 is pending until the box measures its byte orders; the Windows arm is unrun (the box was off)

capability crypto/blake3  the BLAKE3 hash, keyed hash and key derivation, read to any length and from any offset
  heroes  crypto.blake3, crypto.blake3_keyed, crypto.blake3_derive_key, crypto.Blake3State, crypto.start_blake3, crypto.start_blake3_keyed, crypto.start_blake3_derive_key, crypto.update_blake3, crypto.digest_blake3, crypto.digest_blake3_from
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    none of the three standard libraries has BLAKE3; Zig's `std.crypto.hash.Blake3` does, and the yardstick is its behaviour and the BLAKE3 team's own crate's names (`hash`, `keyed_hash`, `derive_key`, `OutputReader::set_position` as `digest_blake3_from`). All 35 cases of the official `test_vectors.json` (fetched 2026-11-17) pass in all three modes to 131 bytes, and Zig answered three of them alike; a key that is not 32 bytes is `invalid`, where the crate's type takes only 32

capability crypto/argon2  a password hashed, or a key derived, by Argon2d, Argon2i or Argon2id, with a secret and associated data
  heroes  crypto.argon2, crypto.Argon2, crypto.Argon2Params, crypto.argon2_low_memory, crypto.argon2_high_memory, crypto.MAX_MEMORY
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 9106's three vectors and eight more cases (two lanes, segments past 128 blocks, memory no multiple of 4 × lanes, a tag past 64 bytes, the smallest memory and tag, version 16) answered as OpenSSL 4.1's `kdf ARGON2*` did on 2026-11-17; the lanes are computed one after another, which answers what threads would. The bounds are RFC 9106's but the memory, which is `MAX_MEMORY` (2 GiB, RFC 9106's first recommendation exactly). Python's standard library has none (`argon2-cffi` is not of it; its profiles `RFC_9106_LOW_MEMORY` and `RFC_9106_HIGH_MEMORY` are the two functions here), nor Go's (`golang.org/x/crypto/argon2`), nor Rust's

capability crypto/bcrypt  a password hashed by bcrypt, its cost and the limit of 72 bytes said
  heroes  crypto.hash_bcrypt, crypto.verify_bcrypt, crypto.parse_bcrypt_cost
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    OpenBSD's and Solar Designer's classic vectors, which Apache's `htpasswd -vb` accepted, verify; Zig's `bcrypt` answered the hashes of five passwords under one salt, and `htpasswd -bnBC` and Zig's `strHash` wrote strings that verify, on 2026-11-17. A password past 72 bytes is `invalid` (Go's `x/crypto` and Python's `bcrypt` 5 refuse it; OpenBSD's cut it in silence), and so is a zero byte, at which OpenBSD's stops and Go's does not. `$2a$`, `$2b$` and `$2y$` are read, `$2b$` written; the whole string is made again and compared in constant time, as OpenBSD's does, so a salt written with stray low bits does not verify. The salt is the caller's, 16 bytes. Go's `bcrypt.MinCost`, `MaxCost` and `DefaultCost` are 4, 31 and 10, the range `hash_bcrypt` states; none of the three standard libraries has bcrypt

capability crypto/password-strings  a password's hash stored as a string and verified against it
  heroes  crypto.hash_argon2, crypto.verify_argon2, crypto.parse_argon2, crypto.hash_bcrypt, crypto.verify_bcrypt, crypto.parse_bcrypt_cost
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Argon2's PHC string (`$argon2id$v=19$m=..,t=..,p=..$salt$tag`, base 64 with no padding) as the reference implementation writes it; strings Zig wrote in the three types verify, a string with no `v=` is version 16 as the reference reads it, and every part read from a stored string is bounded before anything is allocated, its memory by `MAX_MEMORY`. `parse_argon2` and `parse_bcrypt_cost` answer what a hash was made with, which `argon2-cffi`'s `check_needs_rehash` and Go's `bcrypt.Cost` ask. No salt is made here: `crypto` stands on no C, and the salt comes from `random` or `os.random_bytes`. A secret and associated data have no field in the string, so `hash_argon2` takes neither

capability crypto/hotp  a one-time password made from a counter (HOTP), and one received checked within a window of counters
  heroes  crypto.hotp, crypto.verify_hotp, crypto.Otp, crypto.new_otp, crypto.MAX_OTP_WINDOW
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 4226's Appendix D vectors, and the other cases as Python's `hmac` and `struct` answered them on 2026-11-17. The yardstick is Python's pyotp (not on this machine): `HOTP.at` and `HOTP.verify`, which answers a `bool` where `verify_hotp` answers the counter that matched, which RFC 4226's look-ahead resynchronisation needs. Codes of 6 to 10 digits (RFC 4226 asks 6 at least, and its 31 bits fill 10); a key of one byte at least, where RFC 4226 asks 16 and services often hand apps 10; SHA-1, SHA-256 and SHA-512 alone, the truncation reading 20 bytes of the tag. Every counter of the window is computed and compared in constant time through `equals_constant_time`'s work, whatever matched. None of the three standard libraries has HOTP

capability crypto/totp  a one-time password made from a time the caller gives (TOTP), and one received checked within a window of steps
  heroes  crypto.totp, crypto.verify_totp, crypto.MAX_OTP_WINDOW
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 6238's Appendix B, every time by its three digests and seeds. pyotp's `TOTP.at` and `TOTP.verify(valid_window=)` are the yardstick; `verify_totp` answers the step that matched, the nearest to the time's own first, so that the caller refuses a step it has taken already (RFC 6238's section 5.2). No clock is read: the caller gives the seconds since 1970, and T0 is 0, RFC 6238's default; a window above `MAX_OTP_WINDOW` (1000) or below 0 fails `invalid`. None of the three standard libraries has TOTP

capability crypto/otp-uri  the otpauth URI that hands a one-time password's key to an authenticator app
  heroes  crypto.format_hotp_uri, crypto.format_totp_uri
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Google Authenticator's Key Uri Format: the label `issuer:account`, the key in base 32 with no padding (RFC 4648), then `issuer`, `algorithm`, `digits`, and `counter` or `period`, all always written as that format's own example writes them, where pyotp's `provisioning_uri` leaves out a default; the label and the issuer escaped but for RFC 3986's unreserved characters, as Python's `urllib.parse.quote(s, safe="")` answered on 2026-11-17, and a colon in either refused `invalid`. The base 32 and the escape are written in `crypto/internal/otp`, since `bytes` holds base 32 `pending` and `url` stands on this package's own layer; they move to `bytes` once it has base 32. The URI is written, never read

